================================================================================
Thronsen et al. (phase mapping)
================================================================================

The vector-matching method of DiffractoOrientation (PhaseReferenceLibrary.swift, PhaseVectorMatching.swift and the
PhaseVectorMatcher+*.swift and PhaseVectorTypes.swift files split from it), including the decision rule of `.knownVariants` (in
PhaseVectorMatcher+Classify.swift) and its two constants (direct-matrix count 1, residual cutoff 0.07 inverse angstrom), follow:

    E. Thronsen, J. Hjelen, R. Holmestad et al., "Studying GPB zones and precipitates in Al-Cu-Li with scanning precession
    electron diffraction", Ultramicroscopy 255 (2024) 113861, licensed CC BY 4.0
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Changes: re-implemented in Swift from the published description.

No code was copied from the authors' notebook; their repository carries no licence.

================================================================================
Orientation symmetry and IPF keys
================================================================================

NativeIPFKeys.swift ports the native direction folds, samplers and colour keys
from mac4DSTEM c809679c, mac4DSTEM/Core/Analysis/OrientationResult.swift
(GPL-3.0). The application's orientation-map container is not included.
Py4DSTEMIPFKey.swift reimplements the full-mask beam colour part of py4DSTEM
f050d207851f8accd3aa550365baaf1f38230428, crystal_viz.py
plot_orientation_maps (GPL-3.0); its automatic sector bases are generated by
calling that version's orientation_plan. pymatgen PointGroup (2026.9.24 /
core 2026.10.16, MIT) is an oracle for all eleven proper rotation tables;
SciPy Rotation is an oracle for the minimum-angle representative. Apart from the
orix port below (Sources/DiffractoOrientation/Orix), no orix or MTEX code is copied
into the library. tools/pins/orientation-symmetry/pins.py records provenance.

================================================================================
orix (ported code in Sources/DiffractoOrientation/Orix; a reference for checking)
================================================================================

Sources/DiffractoOrientation/Orix is a port of orix 0.15.0's quaternion,
vector, sampling and colour-key code (orix.quaternion, orix.vector,
orix.sampling, orix.projections, orix.plot's DirectionColorKeyTSL,
IPFColorKeyTSL and EulerColorKey; the colour key after MTEX, Nolze and
Hielscher 2016), with diffpy.structure 3.5's Lattice (BSD-3-Clause) in
CrystalLattice.swift. It was written in Electron Microscopy Studio (GPL-3.0,
same author) and moved here (DiffractoKit#179); each file names what it ports
in its first lines. Taken under GPL-3.0.

tools/pins/orix/*-pins.py run orix 0.15.0 as the oracle for the IPF colour key,
direction reduction, disorientation and misorientation (docs/parity.md P26 and
P36); their output is committed as Tests/DiffractoOrientationTests/Fixtures/
orix-*-pins.json. The moved reference bundles under Tests/DiffractoOrientationTests/
Fixtures/orix-sem are orix 0.15.0's numbers written by the app's
tools/parity/sem/emit.py. Two of them (ipf-sdss-ferrite-austenite,
ipf-nickel-ebsd-large) hold the Euler angles of public EBSD maps: orix-data's
sdss_ferrite_austenite.ang (EBSD data by Jarle Hjelen, NTNU, CC BY 4.0) and
kikuchipy's nickel_ebsd_large dataset (Aanes et al., CC BY 4.0).

